### 1. Mathematical Foundation: Characteristic Functions & Power Sets

#In set theory, the power set \\(\mathscr{P}A\\) of a ground set \\(A\\) is #equinumerous to the set of characteristic functions \\(2^A = \{f : A \to #\{0, 1\}\}\\) [1, 2]. 

#For a finite ground set \\(A = \{0, 1, 2, \dots, 255\}\\) containing 256 #elements, every subset \\(S \subseteq A\\) is uniquely identified by its #256-bit characteristic integer [3]:

#\\[s = \sum_{i \in S} 2^i \in [0, 2^{256} - 1]\\]

#* **Disjoint Union Homomorphism:** The disjoint union of two sets \\(S_1 #\cap S_2 = \emptyset\\) corresponds to integer addition \\(s_{\text{union}} #= s_1 + s_2\\) [4, 5].
#* **Cryptographic Point Mapping:** In our engine, every subset \\(S\\) maps #to a unique curve point \\(P_S = [s]B\\) on the Twisted Edwards curve. By #scalar linearity, disjoint set unions map directly to curve point #additions:

#\\[[s_1 \cup s_2]B = [s_1]B + [s_2]B\\]

#---

### 2. Cantor's Diagonalization in 256-Bit Scalar Space

#Cantor’s diagonal argument proves that no set is equinumerous to its power #set (\\(K < 2^K\\)) [6, 7]. Given any list of \\(N\\) subsets represented #as 256-bit characteristic vectors \\(M_0, M_1, \dots, M_{N-1}\\), we #construct a new subset \\(D\\) by flipping the \\(i\\)-th bit along the #diagonal [6]:

#\\[D_i = 1 - M_{i,i}\\]

#The resulting characteristic scalar \\(s_D\\) is guaranteed to differ from #every row \\(M_i\\) in at least the \\(i\\)-th element, constructing a #subset outside the sequence [6].

